A disconnected exercise can model propagation by copying only an inert marker inside a predeclared temporary sandbox. It should never scan hosts, open sockets, execute a copied file, or write outside the sandbox. One simple model is:

sandbox = {"lab-a": ["lab-b", "lab-c"], "lab-b": ["lab-a", "lab-d"], "lab-c": ["lab-a", "lab-d"], "lab-d": ["lab-b", "lab-c"]}
patched = {"lab-a": False, "lab-b": False, "lab-c": True, "lab-d": False}
seen = {"lab-a"}
queue = ["lab-a"]
events = []
while queue and len(events) < 20:
    source = queue.pop(0)
    for peer in sandbox[source]:
        if not patched[peer] and peer not in seen:
            seen.add(peer)
            queue.append(peer)
            events.append({"from": source, "to": peer, "marker": "SIMULATED_EVENT"})

The identifiers are graph labels, not paths, hostnames, or addresses. A real classroom implementation should validate that input contains only a fixed schema, use a deterministic seed if it adds probability, cap nodes and events, and fail closed on values resembling an IP address, URL, credential, or filesystem path. Keep the marker as data in memory or in a temporary directory created for the exercise, and delete it afterward. Do not use a network library, shell command, real credentials, mounted production directory, or executable payload.

Students can compare flat and segmented graphs, patch coverage, alert delay, and a simulated quarantine action. Measure time to first alert, peak queue, total reach, zones crossed, and residual exposure after isolation. Generate synthetic telemetry for repeated connection attempts or simultaneous file changes and write detection rules from that event log. The simulation demonstrates graph reach and containment; a damaging virus would add real discovery, exploitation, remote copying, execution, persistence, and an adverse payload, all of which are deliberately absent here. Any live defensive validation belongs to an authorized provider with its own isolation controls. The instructor should retain the event log only for the class review, then remove it and document that every identifier was synthetic. Students should be able to explain why the absence of sockets and executable files is the essential distinction between this model and a real worm.
